values ('2026-11-24-georgia', 'Non-Disclosure and Non-Compete Agreement (Georgia)', $nda$NON-DISCLOSURE AND NON-COMPETE AGREEMENT
The Kollective Hospitality Group - Georgia

This Agreement is effective on the date of the last signature below ("Effective Date"). It is between The Kollective Hospitality Group ("Company"), acting through its Founder, Bryan Dorsey; Bryan Dorsey individually, solely as to information and intellectual property he owns or controls (together, "Disclosing Party"); and {{SIGNER_NAME}} ("Receiving Party"). They are collectively the "Parties."

1. PURPOSE, CONSIDERATION, AND PRIOR DISCLOSURES
The Parties may evaluate or perform authorized Company activities for {{ENTITY}} and other Company entities, including the duties described in Schedule A, operations, planning, outreach, launch coordination, vendor coordination, and other authorized Company activities ("Purpose"). Use for another business or person is not authorized.
In exchange for Receiving Party's promises, Disclosing Party shall provide the new or continuing benefit identified in Schedule A. Past sharing alone is not represented as new consideration.
Prior and future disclosures. This Agreement covers confidential information disclosed before, on, or after the Effective Date to the extent Receiving Party lawfully received it for authorized Company work, including retained copies. Its duties apply prospectively; signatures are not backdated and existing claims or duties are not released.

2. CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION AND PROTECTED MATERIALS
"Confidential Information" means valuable nonpublic information owned or lawfully controlled by the Disclosing Party, shared for the Purpose in any form, and marked confidential or reasonably understood to be confidential. Marking is not required. It includes business plans, financials, prices, contracts, strategies, unreleased concepts and branding, systems, credentials, and nonpublic customer, vendor, partner, and personnel records. It covers affiliate information actually shared for the Purpose under a lawful confidentiality duty.
Project management systems. Nonpublic ClickUp spaces, dashboards, entity audits, project plans, task assignments, timelines, milestones, dependencies, launch plans, operating procedures, internal notes, status reports, budgets, resource plans, credentials, and performance information.
Enterprise planning materials. Nonpublic business concepts, portfolio priorities, vendor and partner information, contracts, pricing, financial assumptions, staffing plans, product roadmaps, operational issues, risk assessments, and strategic decisions reflected in project-management systems or communications.
Enterprise strategy information. Nonpublic portfolio architecture, unreleased brands and entities, business models, cross-brand strategies, launch sequencing, geographic expansion plans, vertical-integration plans, internal synergies, acquisition or partnership targets, ownership or deal structures, financial assumptions, market-entry plans, and other information showing how the Company intends to coordinate, commercialize, or expand its portfolio.
The "Protected Materials" include the nonpublic selection, organization, annotations, updates, exports, spreadsheets, CRM or project-management records, screenshots, copies, and derived summaries of the foregoing information. Particular information qualifies as a trade secret only if it meets applicable law, including O.C.G.A. Section 10-1-761.
Exclusions. Confidential Information excludes information Receiving Party can establish was already lawfully known without restriction; becomes public without breach of a duty; is lawfully received from an unrestricted third party; or is independently developed without using Confidential Information. Public contact facts do not become private solely by inclusion in a database, but a nonpublic compilation and its relationship intelligence may remain protected. General skills and experience are not restricted by this definition.

3. NON-DISCLOSURE, NON-USE, AND SAFEGUARDS
Receiving Party shall use Confidential Information only for authorized Company work; protect it with reasonable care; and disclose it only to Company-approved persons who need it for the Purpose and are bound by equivalent confidentiality duties. Receiving Party is responsible for breaches by persons to whom Receiving Party authorizes disclosure, except for legally protected disclosures.
Specific protected-material restrictions. Receiving Party shall not sell, license, publish, give away, scrape, bulk export, or copy Protected Materials except as specifically authorized for Company work; merge them into personal or third-party records; upload them to unapproved accounts, services, or AI tools; share credentials; or use them to obtain customers, sponsorships, media coverage, vendor relationships, partnerships, commissions, commercial opportunities, or other benefits for Receiving Party or another business. This includes using nonpublic details recalled from memory or disguised in a reformatted list. Authorized Company work remains permitted.
Receiving Party shall notify Bryan Dorsey of actual or reasonably suspected unauthorized access, use, or disclosure promptly and within 24 hours of discovery; preserve evidence; and reasonably assist containment and recovery. Receiving Party may not conceal an incident or delete relevant evidence.
Compelled disclosure. For a legally compelled disclosure that is not a protected report below, Receiving Party shall, where lawful, give prompt notice, disclose only what is required, and reasonably assist with protective measures at Disclosing Party's expense.
Protected rights. Nothing restricts legally protected reports to government agencies or law enforcement; investigative cooperation; truthful testimony; communications with counsel; discussion of wages, hours, or working conditions; or disclosures of suspected unlawful conduct, discrimination, harassment, or retaliation. No prior permission or notice is required for protected activity, and no lawful whistleblower award is waived.
Federal trade-secret immunity notice. Under 18 U.S.C. Section 1833(b), an individual is immune from federal or state trade-secret civil or criminal liability for a trade-secret disclosure made confidentially to a federal, state, or local official, directly or indirectly, or to an attorney, solely to report or investigate a suspected legal violation; or made in a complaint or other proceeding document filed under seal. In an employer-retaliation lawsuit for reporting a suspected violation, the individual may disclose the secret to counsel and use it in court if documents containing it are filed under seal and further disclosure occurs only by court order. These protections include contractors and consultants and do not authorize otherwise unlawful access to information.

4. LIMITED NON-COMPETE, NON-SOLICITATION, AND NON-CIRCUMVENTION
Definitions and scope. "Termination Date" is the date Receiving Party's actual employment or service engagement with the Company ends, regardless of reason. If no engagement exists, it is the date either Party gives written notice ending discussions and access under this Agreement. "Lookback Period" means the 12 months before the Termination Date, or before the current date while the engagement continues, limited to the actual relationship if shorter.
"Restricted Business" means only the Company's operations for hospitality, events, food and beverage ventures, applications or platforms, or other business lines that the Company actually conducted and in which Receiving Party materially worked during the Lookback Period. It excludes unrelated lines and merely contemplated expansions.
"Restricted Territory" means only those counties within Georgia where the Company actually conducted the relevant Restricted Business and Receiving Party materially served customers, solicited business, or managed operations for that business during the Lookback Period. A home office alone does not establish a service market. Remote activities are covered only when directed into those counties. No restriction automatically covers all of Georgia.
Limited non-compete; statutory eligibility required. During an actual employment or service engagement and for two years after its Termination Date, Receiving Party shall not, within the Restricted Territory, perform or supervise substantially the same duties Receiving Party materially performed for the Company for a business that competes with the Restricted Business, including one Receiving Party owns or operates. Receiving Party may work in an unrelated role or business line and hold a passive investment of up to 2% in a publicly traded company, provided Receiving Party does not otherwise breach this Agreement.
This non-compete applies only if the actual relationship is covered by O.C.G.A. Sections 13-8-51 and 13-8-52 and Receiving Party's actual duties place Receiving Party in a category eligible under Section 13-8-53(a), such as regular customer solicitation or sales, qualifying management, or a legally defined key employee or professional. A title, a contractor label, preliminary discussions, or access to Protected Materials alone does not conclusively establish eligibility. The Parties do not stipulate to eligibility without supporting facts. If these conditions are absent, this non-compete does not apply; independently valid confidentiality and non-use duties remain.
Customer and sponsor non-solicitation. For a relationship governed by Georgia's Restrictive Covenants Act, Receiving Party shall not, during the engagement or for two years after its Termination Date, personally or through another person solicit or attempt to solicit a Covered Customer for products or services competing with those the Company provided. A "Covered Customer" is an actual customer, including a sponsor purchasing Company services, or a specifically and actively pursued prospective customer, with whom Receiving Party dealt, whose dealings Receiving Party supervised, or about whose Company relationship Receiving Party obtained confidential information during the Lookback Period.
A name on a database by itself does not establish a Covered Customer. General advertising not aimed at Covered Customers and acceptance of genuinely unsolicited inquiries are not solicitation under this paragraph; other independently applicable duties remain. A PR contact who is not a customer or active customer prospect is protected through confidentiality and non-use, not a blanket ban on contact.
Non-circumvention of identified opportunities. During the relationship and for two years after the Termination Date, Receiving Party shall not knowingly use Confidential Information to divert to Receiving Party or another person a specific Company customer, vendor, service-provider, technology, venue, manufacturing, distribution, investment, partnership, or project opportunity in the Restricted Territory that the Company was actively pursuing and with which Receiving Party was materially involved during the Lookback Period, by bypassing the Company's participation. This does not prohibit independently developed opportunities, pre-existing relationships, or unrelated dealings that do not use Confidential Information. Section 3 separately protects the Protected Materials regardless of location.

5. INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY, WORK PRODUCT, AND COMPANY SYSTEMS
Disclosing Party retains its respective ownership and lawful control of its Confidential Information, Protected Materials, branding, creative assets, systems, accounts, and other intellectual property. Receiving Party gains only the limited permission to use them for the Purpose. Reformatting, annotating, improving, or adding to Company materials does not give Receiving Party ownership of the underlying materials or permission to retain or reuse them.
Work product and assignment. To the extent permitted by law, original materials created by Receiving Party specifically within the scope of authorized Company work and using Company resources, Confidential Information, or a Company assignment ("Work Product") are specially commissioned for the Company and shall be treated as works made for hire where legally applicable. To the extent any Work Product does not qualify as a work made for hire, Receiving Party hereby assigns to the Company all right, title, and interest Receiving Party may acquire in that Work Product, including associated intellectual-property rights, excluding Pre-Existing Materials identified below. Receiving Party shall reasonably execute further documents needed to confirm Company ownership. Nothing transfers rights the Company does not lawfully own or control.
Pre-existing materials and relationships. "Pre-Existing Materials" means intellectual property, templates, databases, processes, content, contacts, or other materials demonstrably owned or controlled by Receiving Party before the engagement or developed independently without Company Confidential Information. Receiving Party retains ownership of Pre-Existing Materials. If Receiving Party intentionally incorporates Pre-Existing Materials into Company Work Product, Receiving Party grants the Company a perpetual, worldwide, royalty-free license to use, reproduce, modify, distribute, display, perform, and create derivative works from those incorporated materials as necessary to use the Work Product. Material pre-existing assets or relationships expected to be used in Company work should be identified in Schedule A.
Company systems and accounts. Company-created, Company-paid, or Company-controlled domains, websites, social-media accounts, email accounts, CRM records, ClickUp or project-management workspaces, cloud drives, vendor portals, delivery-platform accounts, app-store or developer accounts, advertising accounts, payment or merchant accounts, business phone numbers, credentials, and similar digital assets used for Company operations are Company property to the extent lawfully owned or controlled by the Company. Receiving Party shall not transfer ownership, change recovery information for personal control, lock out authorized Company personnel, delete accounts or business records without authorization, or retain administrator access after authorization ends. Receiving Party shall promptly provide current credentials and reasonable transition assistance upon request or separation.

6. RETURN, DELETION, AND CERTIFICATION
Upon written request, termination of authorized access, or the Termination Date, whichever occurs first, Receiving Party shall stop using Confidential Information and, within five business days, return it or securely delete it as the Company directs. This includes both Protected Materials, exports, screenshots, copies, derivative contact lists, and records in personal devices, email, cloud storage, or other locations under Receiving Party's control. Receiving Party shall identify known third-party recipients and reasonably assist with recovery.
Within the same period Receiving Party shall certify in writing that Receiving Party completed a reasonable search and return or deletion, identifying any legally required retention or inaccessible routine backup. Litigation holds and mandatory preservation take priority over deletion. Retained material must be isolated, remain protected, and not be used for business; routine backups may be deleted through their normal cycle. One copy of this Agreement may be retained for legal records.

7. NO PARTNERSHIP OR EMPLOYMENT CREATED
This Agreement alone creates no partnership, joint venture, employment, agency, equity interest, or obligation to complete a transaction. It does not determine worker classification or promise a fixed engagement. Receiving Party has no authority to bind the Company unless separately authorized in writing.

8. REMEDIES FOR BREACH
Unauthorized disclosure or Protected Materials misuse may cause harm that money alone cannot adequately remedy. An injured Disclosing Party may seek an injunction and other relief, subject to proof and all applicable court requirements, and recover damages legally available. If that party prevails on a material breach claim, it may recover reasonable attorney fees and costs to the extent permitted by law. No automatic penalty, criminal liability, or waiver of required judicial findings is created.

9. GEORGIA LAW AND VENUE
Georgia law governs this Agreement, subject to controlling federal law and any mandatory law that cannot be waived. Subject to lawful jurisdiction and venue requirements, disputes shall be brought in the Georgia state courts in the county identified in Schedule A or the federal district court serving that county. If no county is completed, ordinary Georgia jurisdiction and venue rules apply. Protected agency proceedings are not restricted.

10. TERM AND SURVIVAL
This Agreement takes effect on the Effective Date. Either Party may end further discussions and information exchange by written notice; that alone does not terminate a separate service or employment agreement. Confidentiality and non-use duties survive for as long as the information remains confidential under Section 2, and trade-secret duties survive while the information qualifies as a trade secret. These protections apply without a geographic limitation. Section 4 restrictions expire according to their express two-year periods and are not automatically renewed by this survival clause. Ownership, return obligations, protected rights, and remedies for accrued breaches survive as applicable.

11. ENTIRE AGREEMENT, SEVERABILITY, AND SIGNATURES
This Agreement and Schedule A state the Parties' agreement on their subject matter and supersede prior discussions on it, without releasing accrued claims or superseding a separate signed compensation, service, or intellectual-property agreement. Amendments must be written and signed by the Parties. A failure to enforce once is not a continuing waiver.
Each covenant is separate. A court may sever or narrow an invalid provision to the extent Georgia law permits, but may not make a restriction broader than the Parties agreed. No Party is promised that a court will modify or enforce a restriction. Counterparts and lawful electronic signatures are permitted. Notices may be delivered personally, by traceable delivery, or by email to the contacts in Schedule A; an email notice is effective upon acknowledgment of receipt. Each Party has an opportunity to obtain independent legal advice and receives a signed copy.

